text:
I. The Khevsurians were at the guest-house, beer brewed in the cauldron;
They sat upon the flat roofs and feasted, drinking it from bowls;
Some played the panduri, singing loudly to its tune,
Laying heroic tales as a bridge to the listeners' feelings;
With fervor, they recited the sacred names of heroes in verse;
The elders smoked their pipes, smoke wrapped around them like mist,
They recounted their deeds, offering blessings for the heroes.
To instruct the young, they remembered them with praise:
"It is for you to show, who among you shall prove to be a man from this day forth!"

Swift as a bullet, mystical as a snake,
All of Pshav-Khevsureti deifies him and sees him as solace,
The crowned Tamar is always saying this:
“As long as I have Mindia with me, and with him the mountain folk,
No matter how hard the enemy tries, they can do me no harm.”
He knows the tricks to defeat and crush the enemy;
If he reaches the wounded while they still live, let them have no fear of death.
His peerless medicine will heal even one cut in two;
Thus his numbers do not dwindle in wars, he always has a vast army;
The tale of his adventures has traveled far and wide.

From the house is heard bitterness, the sound of arguing, of wrath;
There are not many people—the tone of conversation of two.
Upon the embers a fire is lit, kindled with wood;
On that side a woman is seen beside the children,
On this side a lone Khevsur laments much with a sigh.

Indeed I see that the water brings people, shields, and swords;
In a thousand places fortresses crumble, houses stand by stone walls;
To such wretchedness, only a mourner is missing.
As if we were not in trouble, as if we were unharmed,
In the end I saw that we too were cast out with our home and hearth.
A torrent rushed from above, tore the foundation from the fortress
And mixed it too down with the shingle of the Aragvi.
It took the house with its roof, and swept us along too;
I want to cry out – help me! – but I could no longer move my tongue.
I pressed the children to my heart, begging the Icon-God for help.
Meanwhile I tried to get out, struggling toward the edge;
I cover the children's faces with the veil of a black kerchief.
And as many times as I reached the shore, so many times it was harsh;
The faces of men met me there, black as pitch.
They pushed me, threw me back into the water, and shouted this:
"Where are you coming? Go, follow the water, it is the command of God!"
